<p>In 1909, {% person "william-a-lanterman" %}W. A. Lanterman{% endperson %}, {% person "lyman-northrop-cary" %}L. N. Cary{% endperson %}, {% person "charles-lewis-c-l-timmerman" %}C. L. Timmerman{% endperson %}, E. H. Hugh, and {% person "theodore-a-cummins" %}T. A. Cummins{% endperson %} led a weeklong fundraising drive to establish a modern hospital in 29-year-old Mandan. The successful campaign created a stock company capitalized at {% inflation 50000, 1909 %}, with 100 stockholders, including 50 area farmers.</p>

<p>Stockholders announced in August 1909 that the hospital would be built in the Heartview Addition. The meeting was held in the Business Men's Club rooms at the InterOcean Hotel. Other locations considered were in the First Northern Pacific Addition and the Helmsworth and McLean's Addition.</p>

<p>The <em>Mandan Pioneer</em> praised the south-facing hillside location on the western edge of town as a suitable site for the proposed {% inflation 30000, 1909 %} public building. In October 1909, the construction contract was awarded to C. E. Wierschke of Glenwood, Wisconsin, for {% inflation 22400, 1909 %}. Mandan's first formal hospital, a three-story brick building, was completed in 1910 and opened June 11.</p>

