    <figcaption>Chamber of Commerce aerial photograph, 1955</figcaption>
  </figure>
</div>

<p>A new annex and nurses' home were dedicated in May 1936. The {% inflation 12500, 1936 %} expansion accommodated Mandan's growing population and helped ensure adequate nursing staff. Control of the institution and building later passed to the City of Mandan, which operated it as the nonprofit Mandan Hospital.</p>

<div class="image-gallery">
  <figure>
    <img src="/assets/images/gone-forever/deaconess-hospital/708-1963-color-hospital-kms.jpg" alt="Artist's rendering of Mandan Hospital around 1963">
    <figcaption>Artist's rendering of Mandan Hospital, circa 1963</figcaption>
  </figure>
</div>

<p>In June 1961, Mandan voters approved a {% inflation 400000, 1961 %} bond issue for a new hospital with 91.8 percent voting yes. Initially planned as a 56-bed, {% inflation 950000, 1961 %} facility, it opened September 9, 1963, with 63 beds. A five-physician clinic was added in 1964, and an interchange connected the site with the newly constructed four-lane Interstate 94 in 1965.</p>

<p>Heartview Alcoholism Treatment Clinic purchased the former hospital and adjacent nurses' home in November 1963 and converted the complex into a 40-bed facility offering inpatient and outpatient care. As the only institution of its kind between Minneapolis and Seattle, Heartview expanded both its services and capacity. A 1978 addition increased inpatient capacity to 76 beds but required demolition of the original hospital. A 16-bed adolescent unit completed in 1986 brought the complex to a peak of 91 inpatient beds.</p>

<p>Heartview served patients from across the United States and Canada. Financial difficulties eventually forced the Heartview Foundation to downsize, and the program moved to Bismarck in 1996. HIT Inc. purchased the building and continued expanding services for people with physical or developmental disabilities. HIT moved to a larger northern Mandan facility in 2012. The former hospital property was subsequently owned by North Dakota Teen Challenge.</p>
